issues.body:
#### Technical Details

[`MultiFarmStrategy._checkDepositTarget()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L404) verifies that the target configuration is enabled and that its farm, pool and add-liquidity target match the supplied `depositPosition`. It does not verify that the NFT is still owned by the Sickle or its owner, or staked on behalf of the Sickle.

The configuration is keyed by `(sickle, nftManager, tokenId)` and persists when the NFT leaves the Sickle. For example, [`NftFarmStrategy.simpleWithdraw()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L494) withdraws and transfers the NFT to the owner without clearing the multi-compound configuration. The NFT may also subsequently be transferred outside the protocol.

Only the Sickle owner can call [`setNftConfig()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/MultiCompoundSettingsRegistry.sol#L76) or [`unsetNftConfig()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/MultiCompoundSettingsRegistry.sol#L88). Therefore, this scenario requires the user to transfer the target NFT without first removing its automation configuration; the keeper cannot independently configure an unrelated target.

When `compoundInPlace` is true, [`MultiFarmStrategy._nftCompoundMultiple()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L237) skips both [`MultiFarmStrategy._withdrawNft()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L520) and [`MultiFarmStrategy._depositNft()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L547) and directly calls [`NftZapLib.zapIn()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/NftZapLib.sol#L26). Adding liquidity to an existing position is normally permissionless, so this call can still succeed after the NFT has been transferred to a different owner.

#### Impact

Low. Rewards harvested from several Sickle positions can be irreversibly donated to the current owner of an NFT that no longer belongs to the Sickle. The loss can include all rewards selected by an authorized keeper for the multi-compound operation. The loss is conditional on the Sickle owner leaving the configuration enabled after transferring the NFT.

#### Recommendation

Add the following error at contract level:

```solidity
error DepositPositionNotControlled();
```

Then insert this check in [`MultiFarmStrategy._checkDepositTarget()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L404), after binding the zap target to `depositPosition`:

```solidity
address nftOwner =
    depositPosition.nft.ownerOf(depositPosition.tokenId);

if (
    nftOwner != address(sickle)
        && nftOwner != sickle.owner()
) {
    address farmConnector = connectorRegistry.connectorOf(
        depositPosition.farm.stakingContract
    );

    if (
        !INftFarmConnector(farmConnector).isStaked(
            address(sickle), depositPosition
        )
    ) {
        revert DepositPositionNotControlled();
    }
}
```

This permits a target held by the Sickle, held directly by [`sickle.owner()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/base/SickleStorage.sol#L29), or reported by the farm connector as staked for the Sickle.

#### Developer Response

Fixed in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#894, commit 06f95f90.

- `MultiFarmStrategy._checkDepositTarget()` requires current custody: the Sickle holds the NFT, its owner holds it, or the farm reports it staked for that Sickle.
- Follow-up in #899 (202af112) and #901 (fea13fda, 191e88c4): four connectors answered "staked by anyone" rather than binding the caller. Sushi now uses `getStaker`; Thena/Atlantis/Blackhole and Ultrasolid use `nft.ownerOf`.
- Hybra's gauge publishes no tokenId→depositor record so it stays token-level. A stale target there is rejected by the gauge's own withdraw authorisation instead.
- Added regression tests, including one that fails against the previous connector behaviour.

#### Technical Details

[`Automation._executeOne()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/Automation.sol#L739) measures gas only around the inner strategy call:

```solidity
// contracts/Automation.sol
uint256 gasBefore = gasleft();
(bool success, bytes memory result) =
    strategy.call{ value: value }(data);
uint256 gasUsed = gasBefore - gasleft();

// ...
_chargeGasAccount(sickle, permission, gasUsed, data);
```

[`ArbitrumGasCostAdapter.txCost()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/GasCostAdapter.sol#L159) then charges only that measured gas plus a fixed overhead:

```solidity
// contracts/GasCostAdapter.sol
function txCost(
    uint256 l2GasUsed,
    bytes calldata
) external view returns (uint256) {
    return _executionCost(l2GasUsed);
}
```

On Arbitrum Nitro, the L1 poster cost is converted to L2 gas units and deducted from the transaction's available gas before EVM execution starts. It is therefore included in transaction-level gas accounting, but not in a `gasleft()` delta measured around an inner contract call. This behavior is visible in Nitro's [transaction processor](https://github.com/OffchainLabs/nitro/blob/master/arbos/tx_processor.go#L442-L517), where `posterGas` is calculated and charged before the EVM is entered.

The adapter also ignores its `bytes calldata` argument. A sufficiently large fixed `overhead` may partially or fully compensate for the omitted poster fee under particular conditions, but it cannot track changes in calldata compressibility or the L1 base fee and therefore cannot guarantee an accurate reimbursement.

#### Impact

Low. The adapter does not necessarily under-reimburse every operation: the configured `overhead` may offset or exceed the omitted costs. When it is lower than the unmeasured execution costs plus the poster fee, the keeper is under-reimbursed and may stop executing economically unviable automation. When it is higher, the Sickle is overcharged. Because the poster fee varies with calldata compressibility and L1 pricing while `overhead` is fixed, the reimbursement cannot consistently match the keeper's actual cost.

#### Recommendation

Keep `overhead` for the fixed or marginal execution costs outside the metering window, but account for the variable poster fee separately using the supported Arbitrum gas precompile, such as `ArbGasInfo`, or redesign settlement so that transaction-wide costs can be reconciled after execution. Do not calibrate a fixed `overhead` as a substitute for the poster fee.

For batched automation, charge the poster fee once and allocate it across the operations; querying and charging the entire transaction fee once per item would overcharge batches. Add an Arbitrum fork test that compares total keeper cost with the total amount reimbursed.

#### Developer Response

Not fixable at this layer. Documented in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#894, commit d4701c3b.

- The executing contract cannot reconstruct the signed RLP it would need to price the transaction.
- `docs/AutomationKeeperTrust.md` records the gap and what closing it requires: the keeper computes the transaction-level figure off-chain and submits it, bounded on-chain.

#### Technical Details

[`NftFarmStrategy.exitFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L647) validates the automation settings for the supplied `position`, then forwards independently supplied withdrawal parameters.

[`NftFarmStrategy._exit()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L814) eventually calls [`NftFarmStrategy._withdraw()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L721), which withdraws the validated position from its farm but passes the unrelated `withdrawParams` to [`NftFarmStrategy._zapOut()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L1073).

[`NftZapLib.zapOut()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/NftZapLib.sol#L107) selects the connector and removes liquidity according to `params.zap.removeLiquidityParams.nft` and `tokenId`. Neither value is compared with the position that passed the settings check.

[`NftFarmStrategy.rebalanceFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L670) has the same problem. Its pre-mint checks bind the requested new target and range, but do not require the remove-liquidity source in `params.withdraw` to equal `params.position`.

Thus, permission to automate position `A` can be used to remove liquidity from another NFT `B` held by the same Sickle, even if `B` has no corresponding exit or rebalance consent.

#### Impact

Low. An authorized keeper can forcibly remove or reallocate liquidity belonging to another Sickle-owned NFT. This can:

- close or materially alter a position for which automation was not enabled;
- realize fees and losses at an unintended time;
- execute caller-selected swaps and incur slippage;
- leave the authorized position withdrawn but otherwise unchanged.

#### Recommendation

Before automated exit or rebalance execution, require:

```solidity
NftRemoveLiquidity calldata remove =
    withdrawParams.zap.removeLiquidityParams;

if (
    remove.nft != position.nft
        || remove.tokenId != position.tokenId
) {
    revert WithdrawPositionMismatch();
}
```

#### Developer Response

Fixed in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#894, commit 0f712546.

- Rebalance binds the removal source in `ShiftMath._checkRebalanceTargetBindingsInner()`, alongside the existing target checks, so the strategy pays no bytecode.
- `exitFor()` binds inline — there is no ShiftMath call on that path, and adding one for two comparisons costs an external call on every automated exit.
- Added regression tests for both.

#### Technical Details

The strategy defines distinct `Harvest` and `HarvestFor` fee selectors. The test configuration also assigns different rates to them: `90` and `120` respectively.

Although [`NftFarmStrategy.harvestFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L591) correctly calls [`NftFarmStrategy._harvest()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L732) with `HarvestFor`, the fee argument is discarded whenever swaps are present:

```solidity
function _harvest(
    Sickle sickle,
    NftPosition calldata position,
    NftHarvest calldata params,
    bytes4 fee
) private {
    if (params.swaps.length > 0) {
        _claimAndSwap(sickle, position, params);
    } else {
        _claim(sickle, position, params.harvest, fee);
    }
    // ...
}
```

[`NftFarmStrategy._claimAndSwap()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L998) hardcodes the manual selector:

```solidity
data[2] = abi.encodeCall(
    IFeesLib.chargeFees,
    (address(this), NftFarmStrategyFees.Harvest, params.outputTokens)
);
```

As a result, automated [`harvestFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L591) and [`rebalanceFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L670) operations containing swaps pay the `Harvest` fee rather than `HarvestFor`.

In addition, [`NftFarmStrategy._exit()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L814) always invokes [`NftFarmStrategy._harvest()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L732) with `Harvest`, even when it is reached through [`NftFarmStrategy.exitFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L647).

#### Impact

Informational.

#### Recommendation

Pass the selected fee through every helper:

```solidity
if (params.swaps.length > 0) {
    _claimAndSwap(sickle, position, params, fee);
} else {
    _claim(sickle, position, params.harvest, fee);
}
```

Similarly, make [`_exit()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L814) accept a harvest-fee argument and pass `HarvestFor` from [`exitFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L647) while preserving `Harvest` for manual exits.

#### Developer Response

Fixed in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#894, commit 65970bf9.

- Automated harvest-with-swaps and `exitFor()` now charge `HarvestFor` rather than the manual selector.

[`ShiftMath._formulaTicksForRebalance()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L138-L164) derives the exact range an automator must mint, but never checks that the resulting position clears the user's rebalance trigger. With a supported negative buffer the target is a fixed point: the same position is withdrawn and re-minted to identical ticks at an unchanged pool tick. Because the shift check rejects every range except the formula's output, the automator cannot avoid this by choosing a better target.

#### Technical Details

[`NftSettingsRegistry.validateRebalanceFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol#L161-L195) permits a rebalance whenever the tick lies outside the buffered interval `[tickLower - bufferTicksBelow, tickUpper + bufferTicksAbove)`:

```solidity
// contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol:182-188
if (
    poolInfo.tick
        >= positionInfo.tickLower - int24(config.bufferTicksBelow)
        && poolInfo.tick
            < positionInfo.tickUpper + int24(config.bufferTicksAbove)
) {
    revert TickWithinRange();
}
```

Negative buffers are a documented feature — [`RebalanceConfig`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/structs/NftSettingsStructs.sol#L27-L62) states _"Can be negative (rebalance before position goes above range)"_ — and [`_checkRebalanceConfig()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol#L401-L443) bounds them only by `±2 * MAX_TICK`. They pull the trigger _inside_ the position, so a position can be eligible while the tick still sits within its own range.

[`preMintRebalanceChecks()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L210-L230) re-derives that trigger to choose a direction, then computes the target with formulas that only preserve the old width and offset the range by the signed entry value:

```solidity
// contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol:182-204
expectedLower = tickFloor - cfg.entryTickSpacesUp * spacing;
expectedUpper = expectedLower + width;

// or

expectedUpper = tickFloor + (cfg.entryTickSpacesDown + 1) * spacing;
expectedLower = expectedUpper - width;
```

Neither [`_formulaTicksUp()` nor `_formulaTicksDown()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L182-L204) checks whether the tick falls inside the _new_ buffered interval. On the WBTC/USDC 0.3% pool used by the existing fork suite — `tickSpacing = 60`, width 3 tick-spaces, `entryTickSpacesDown = 1`, `bufferTicksBelow = -120`:

|                   | old position       | after shift              |
| ----------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------ |
| range             | `[70800, 70980)`   | `[70680, 70860)`         |
| stay-put interval | `[70920, 70980)`   | `[70800, 70860)`         |
| tick `70785`      | outside → eligible | outside → still eligible |

The replacement's own lower trigger is `70680 + 120 = 70800`, and `70785 < 70800`, so it is eligible the moment it is minted — while the tick sits inside its range, earning fees. Re-running the formula against `[70680, 70860)` returns `[70680, 70860)`.

The numbers are not special. With `W` the width in tick-spaces and `r = tick - floor(tick, ts)`, a shifted-down target stays past its own lower trigger whenever `bufferTicksBelow < (down + 1 - W) * ts - r`, with the symmetric relation for the up direction; `-120` satisfies it for every `r`. With zero buffers the relation reduces to an entry value that places the position entirely outside the tick, and [`ShiftConfig`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/structs/ShiftConfigStructs.sol#L44-L49) accepts every signed `int24` for both entry fields and is validated nowhere.

Such configurations matter because shift removes the automator's discretion over the target. Without a shift payload, [`_shiftSetupInner()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L279-L332) returns `isShift = false` and the keeper picks the new ticks itself, so a reasonable one chooses a range that clears the trigger. With shift enabled, [`ShiftMismatch` rejects any range other than the formula's output](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L317-L329). The automator's only options become "mint the retriggering range" or "do not rebalance at all".

Nothing interrupts the resulting cycle. [`NftFarmStrategy._rebalance()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L841-L880) mints a new NFT and migrates the settings through [`transferNftSettings()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol#L295-L331), preserving the exact condition that caused the first rebalance; [`_checkTickWidth()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol#L469-L490) re-runs on migration but passes, because the formula preserves the width. [`postMintRebalanceChecks()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L235-L253) verifies only the minted pool identity and exact tick equality. `RebalanceConfig.delayMin` ("Delay in minutes before rebalancing") exists as a struct field and NatSpec but no contract reads it, so there is no on-chain cooldown either.

#### Proof of concept

Add both of the following to `test/strategies/ShiftRebalance.t.sol`, the existing WBTC/USDC mainnet-fork suite. The test drives two consecutive `Automation.rebalanceFor()` calls through the approved automator and asserts the pool tick never moves between them. The helper wraps the file's existing `_buildShiftRebalance()` to override the mock-swap input and the mint caps, so the second call can be funded from what the first left; the geometry it builds is unchanged.

```solidity
function _buildShiftRebalanceSized(
    uint256 tokenId,
    int24 newLower,
    int24 newUpper,
    uint256 swapAmountIn,
    uint256 amount0Desired,
    uint256 amount1Desired
) private returns (NftRebalance memory params) {
    params = _buildShiftRebalance(tokenId, newLower, newUpper);
    params.increase.zap.swaps[0].amountIn = swapAmountIn;
    params.increase.zap.addLiquidityParams.amount0Desired = amount0Desired;
    params.increase.zap.addLiquidityParams.amount1Desired = amount1Desired;
}

function test_shift_same_range_rebalance_repeats() public prank {
    bytes memory extraData = abi.encode(
        ShiftConfig({
            magic: SHIFT_MAGIC,
            enabled: true,
            entryTickSpacesUp: 1,
            entryTickSpacesDown: 1
        })
    );
    int24 spacing = contracts.pool.tickSpacing();
    uint256 tokenId = _depositAndOptIn(extraData, -2 * spacing, 0);

    (int24 oldLower, int24 oldUpper,) = _get_range_and_liquidity(tokenId);
    int24 width = oldUpper - oldLower;

    _pushTickBelow(oldLower);
    int24 tick = _get_tick(contracts.pool);

    int24 expectedUpper = _floorToSpacing(tick, spacing) + 2 * spacing;
    int24 expectedLower = expectedUpper - width;

    vm.stopPrank();
    vm.startPrank(ctx.approvedAutomator);

    _callRebalanceFor(
        _buildShiftRebalanceSized(
            tokenId, expectedLower, expectedUpper, 0.05e8, 0.1e8, 10_000e6
        )
    );
    uint256 secondTokenId = _get_token_id();
    assertGt(secondTokenId, tokenId, "first rebalance did not mint");

    (int24 l2, int24 u2,) = _get_range_and_liquidity(secondTokenId);
    assertEq(l2, expectedLower);
    assertEq(u2, expectedUpper);

    // The zap swaps through the mock router, so the real pool tick is
    // untouched: the second call sees exactly the state the first left.
    assertEq(_get_tick(contracts.pool), tick, "pool tick moved");

    _callRebalanceFor(
        _buildShiftRebalanceSized(
            secondTokenId, expectedLower, expectedUpper,
            0.01e8, 0.02e8, 2000e6
        )
    );
    uint256 thirdTokenId = _get_token_id();
    assertGt(thirdTokenId, secondTokenId, "second rebalance did not mint");

    (int24 l3, int24 u3,) = _get_range_and_liquidity(thirdTokenId);
    assertEq(l3, l2, "lower tick changed");
    assertEq(u3, u2, "upper tick changed");
}
```

#### Impact

Medium. An honest automator scanning for eligible positions can re-execute the same rebalance because each successful call leaves the next one valid, and the shift constraint denies it any alternative target. Every subsequent iteration withdraws and re-mints the full position while ending at the same ticks from which that iteration started, can charge the configured rebalance fee against the withdrawn principal and `HarvestFor` against accrued rewards, and may incur swap, slippage and prepaid-gas costs. At an unchanged tick the cycle continues until the user disables automation, a cutoff is crossed, the available balance is depleted, or the keeper suppresses another otherwise-valid execution.

#### Recommendation

Reject the offending configuration rather than the individual rebalance. If a configuration violates the static bounds below, at least one tick remainder produces an immediately eligible target; where the relation holds for every remainder, so does every target calculated at its unchanged source tick. A per-call check therefore makes the affected shift direction unavailable for every tick remainder when the corresponding every-remainder bound is violated, and unavailable only at the offending remainders otherwise — trading a fee drain for an automation outage whenever that direction is triggered.

Validate the shift payload against the buffers and width when settings are stored, in [`checkConfigValues`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol#L335-L371) alongside the existing `_checkTickWidth()` — decoding `ShiftConfig` only when `extraData` carries `SHIFT_MAGIC`, and inside the `settings.autoRebalance` branch, where the width in tick-spaces is already available as `tickSpacesBelow + tickSpacesAbove + 1`. Requiring the target to sit inside its buffered interval for every tick remainder yields four bounds: `bufferTicksBelow >= -up * ts`, `bufferTicksAbove >= (up - W + 1) * ts`, `bufferTicksBelow >= (down + 1 - W) * ts`, and `bufferTicksAbove >= -down * ts`. Evaluate them in `int256`: unrestricted signed entry values are multiplied by the tick spacing before the invalid configuration is rejected.

As defence in depth for settings stored before that validation exists, add the exact per-call check to [`_shiftSetupInner()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L313-L315) immediately after the target is derived, so an unsafe target is rejected before harvest, withdrawal, swaps or minting consume gas:

```solidity
int256 lowerTrigger = int256(expectedLower)
    - int256(settings.rebalanceConfig.bufferTicksBelow);
int256 upperTrigger = int256(expectedUpper)
    + int256(settings.rebalanceConfig.bufferTicksAbove);
int256 tick = int256(poolInfo.tick);

if (tick < lowerTrigger || tick >= upperTrigger) {
    revert ShiftTargetStillEligible(
        poolInfo.tick, expectedLower, expectedUpper
    );
}
```

The widening matters here even though the equivalent addition in `validateRebalanceFor()` cannot overflow: there the operand is a real position tick bounded by `MAX_TICK`, whereas `expectedLower`/`expectedUpper` are derived from unvalidated signed entry values and are not.

This pre-mint guard addresses the fixed point at the sampled tick but cannot guarantee the final-state invariant, since zap swaps may subsequently move the pool tick. `_verifyShiftTicksInner()` already compares the minted ticks against pre-mint-computed values, so that staleness should not be assumed safe: either derive the target after price-changing swaps, prevent the zap from moving the configured pool, or add a post-mint check as a backstop. A post-mint revert rolls back fees and swaps atomically but burns the gas of the full call, so it should not be the primary validation.

If deliberately out-of-range partial shifts must remain supported, do not relax these checks — instead record the handled trigger on the migrated settings and require demonstrable price progress before the next rebalance. Wiring up the unused `RebalanceConfig.delayMin` would bound the loop rate but would not remove the fixed point.

#### Developer Response

Fixed in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#894, commit 496d83c3, rejecting the formula's fixed point (`formula(T) = T`) so a target identical to the position it replaces is refused, which breaks the repeat cycle while leaving the first genuine shift intact.

- The recommended config-time bounds include `bufferTicksBelow >= -up * ts`. With `entryTickSpacesUp = 0` that reduces to `bufferTicksBelow >= 0`.
- `test_shift_lower_last_band_succeeds` (`test/strategies/ShiftRebalance.t.sol:449`) sets `entryTickSpacesUp/Down = 0` and `bufferTicksBelow = oldLower - tick - 1`, which is negative for an in-range position.
- So that configuration violates the bound and would be rejected at write time, which is why we did not take the recommendation — the negative buffer is what makes the trigger fire while the tick is still in range, and that is the behaviour the config is exercising deliberately.

#### yAudit Follow-up

We agree that the proposed config-time bounds would prohibit intended in-range triggering, so we withdraw that recommendation. However, we do not think `496d83c3` closes every repeat cycle. `_formulaTicksForRebalance()` also selects a direction from `oldPos`, which changes after each rebalance. At a fixed tick, the two possible targets are:

```text
U = [F - up*ts, F - up*ts + W*ts)
D = [F + (down+1-W)*ts, F + (down+1)*ts)
```

These targets are equal only when `W == up + down + 1`. This holds for the original configuration, but it is not enforced generally.

For example:

```text
tick = 100000, F = 99960, ts = 60
W = 2, up = down = 0
bufferBelow = -41, bufferAbove = -20

D = [99900, 100020), stay = [99941, 100000)
tick crosses the upper trigger -> U

U = [99960, 100080), stay = [100001, 100060)
tick crosses the lower trigger -> D
```

Both stay intervals are non-empty and every target differs from the position it replaces. The sequence therefore remains `D -> U -> D -> ...`, while `ShiftTargetUnchanged` never fires.

The current check covers fixed points, but not this two-range cycle. A one-step look-ahead would cover the remaining unchanged-tick case: after deriving the target, project that range as the next `oldPos` and reject the shift if the next applicable target returns to the original range. Non-applicability of the projected position should be treated as a valid terminating shift.

At a fixed tick, only `U` and `D` can be produced, so the existing fixed-point check plus this two-cycle check covers every successful repeat cycle without imposing config-time restrictions on intended in-range triggering.

