`compoundFor()` lets the keeper choose `inPlace`. On an unstaked Slipstream position, `false` turns an idempotent no-op withdrawal into an unconditional redeposit, bypassing the separate stake-mode controller.

#### Technical Details

The [`NftFarmStrategy.compoundFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L614-L637) function accepts a keeper-controlled `inPlace` parameter that determines whether an NFT position will be withdrawn before zapping and redeposited afterward. When `inPlace` is false, [`_compound()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L783-L812) calls [`_withdrawNft()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L975-L995) before the zap and [`_depositNft()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L953-L973) afterward, without checking whether the NFT was staked before the operation or verifying that the final custody state matches the initial state.

This design conflicts with the `StakeStrategy` module, which enforces custody-mode transitions based on on-chain yield ratio signals, threshold checks, and configured dwell periods. The [`SlipstreamGaugeConnector.withdrawNft()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/connectors/velodrome/SlipstreamGaugeConnector.sol#L22-L34) implementation is intentionally idempotent: when an NFT is not staked in the gauge, the connector checks `stakedContains()` and skips the withdrawal rather than reverting. When the keeper calls `compoundFor()` with `inPlace=false` while the NFT is Sickle-custodied (unstaked), the pre-zap withdrawal succeeds silently without moving the NFT, the zap modifies liquidity on the Sickle-held position, and `_depositNft()` unconditionally deposits the NFT into the gauge. The operation completes successfully but changes custody mode rather than preserving it, bypassing all ratio, threshold, and dwell enforcement in [`StakeStrategy.stakeFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/StakeStrategy.sol#L174-L182).

The call requires that the Sickle holds usable pool-token balances to produce nonzero added liquidity. Residual fees or dust from previous operations can satisfy this requirement. Existing validation in [`NftSettingsRegistry.validateCompoundFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/NftSettingsRegistry.sol#L138-L148) and [`ShiftMath.checkCompoundTargetBindings()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/ShiftMath.sol#L115-L128) protects NFT manager and token ID binding but does not constrain the keeper-supplied `inPlace` value or enforce custody-mode consistency.

#### Impact

Low. This outcome does not require a malicious keeper or deliberately malformed parameters. Slipstream compounds normally use `inPlace = false` while the position is gauge-staked. If `StakeStrategy` subsequently performs a legitimate transition to the unstaked mode, an ordinary compound that reuses this configured value observes an idempotent no-op withdrawal and then unconditionally deposits the NFT back into the gauge. The compound therefore reverses the controller's on-chain decision without satisfying its ratio, threshold, or dwell conditions.

Vault shareholders may lose the incremental swap-fee yield expected during the intended unstaked interval and incur unnecessary gas from alternating custody transitions. Principal remains withdrawable, and a later qualifying `unstakeFor()` sequence can restore the intended mode, although its signal and dwell period must be established again. Routine compounds can repeatedly shorten unstaked intervals until the execution path derives `inPlace` from current custody. The harm is therefore bounded and recoverable but can systematically undermine the configured yield-optimization policy, supporting Low severity.

#### Recommendation

Query the farm connector's `isStaked(address(sickle), position)` before executing the compound operation. Derive the execution shape from the current custody state rather than accepting the keeper-provided `inPlace` parameter: if the NFT is unstaked, perform the zap in place and do not call `depositExistingNft()`; if it is staked and the connector requires withdrawal, withdraw and restore the same staked state. After the operation, query `isStaked()` again and assert that the result matches the pre-call snapshot. Any mismatch should revert the entire claim, zap, fee charge, and transfer sequence. This approach preserves custody mode as an invariant and prevents the keeper from overriding the owner's configured stake controller policy.

```diff
function _compound(
    Sickle sickle,
    NftPosition calldata position,
    NftCompound calldata params,
    bool inPlace,
    address[] calldata sweepTokens,
    bytes4 fee
) private {
+   // Snapshot custody state before compounding
+   address connector = connectorRegistry.connectorOf(position.farm.stakingContract);
+   bool wasStaked = INftFarmConnector(connector).isStaked(address(sickle), position);
+
    _claim(sickle, position, params.harvest, fee);

-   if (!inPlace) {
+   // Withdraw only if the NFT was initially staked
+   if (wasStaked) {
        _withdrawNft(sickle, position, params.harvest.extraData);
    }

    _zapIn(sickle, params.zap);

-   if (!inPlace) {
+   // Redeposit only if the NFT was initially staked
+   if (wasStaked) {
        _depositNft(sickle, position, params.harvest.extraData);
    }
+
+   // Assert custody state is preserved
+   bool isStaked = INftFarmConnector(connector).isStaked(address(sickle), position);
+   if (isStaked != wasStaked) revert CustodyModeChanged();

    _sweep(sickle, sweepTokens);

    emit SickleCompoundedNft(
        sickle,
        position.nft,
        position.tokenId,
        position.farm.stakingContract,
        position.farm.poolIndex
    );
}
```

If some connectors cannot reliably expose custody state, store an owner-selected custody policy in the settings registry and enforce it per integration rather than trusting an unauthenticated keeper boolean.

#### Developer Response

Acknowledged as part of the keeper trust mode.

`PangolinV3Connector.claim()` passes `msg.sender` as the PNG recipient. Because the connector executes by delegatecall from a Sickle, `msg.sender` is the calling strategy rather than the Sickle, so claimed rewards accumulate at the strategy contract and cannot be processed or swept for the user.

This is a technically valid Medium-severity bug, but its root cause and required fix are in `PangolinV3Connector`, which is outside the listed audit scope.

#### Technical Details

[`NftFarmStrategy._claim()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/NftFarmStrategy.sol#L1032-L1063) and [`MultiFarmStrategy._harvestNftPositions()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L487-L518) resolve the relevant farm connector and ask the Sickle to execute its `claim()` function through [`Sickle.multicall()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/base/Multicall.sol#L42-L81). The Sickle uses `delegatecall`, so connector code runs with the Sickle as `address(this)` while preserving the strategy contract as `msg.sender`.

[`PangolinV3Connector.claim()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/connectors/pangolin/PangolinV3Connector.sol#L29-L46) first reads the position's accrued `rewardOwed`. When the value is non-zero, it calls:

```solidity
IPangolinPositionManager(address(position.nft)).claimReward(
    position.tokenId, msg.sender
);
```

The external call originates from the Sickle and therefore passes the position manager's ownership authorization, but the independently supplied recipient is the strategy address. The position's PNG entitlement is consumed and the tokens are transferred to `NftFarmStrategy` or `MultiFarmStrategy`, not to the Sickle.

Subsequent fee, zap, and sweep delegatecalls operate in the Sickle context and inspect only the Sickle's balances. They cannot process PNG held directly by the strategy. Neither strategy exposes a token-recovery function, so the affected claim can complete and emit normal events while its PNG remains inaccessible to the user.

##### Proof of concept

