`MultiFarmStrategy` binds each multi-compound source to a destination through `MultiCompoundConfig.target` and `targetPoolId`. This identifies the target pool for V4-style positions, but not for bare V3 position managers: every pool under the manager shares the same `Farm`, while the V3 `targetPoolId` is always zero. An authorized keeper can therefore consolidate rewards from a source configured under one V3 pool into an owner-enabled target position belonging to another pool under the same manager.

#### Technical Details

[`nftCompoundMultipleFor()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L218-L235) first validates the keeper-selected deposit position and then passes its `farm` and derived `targetPoolId` to every source check:

```solidity
bytes32 targetPoolId = _checkDepositTarget(sickle, params);
_validateMultiCompoundFor(
    sickle,
    params.claims,
    params.nftClaims,
    params.depositPosition.farm,
    targetPoolId
);
```

[`_checkDepositTarget()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L404-L438) correctly requires the candidate NFT to have an owner-written opt-in and binds the zap to that candidate's manager and `tokenId`. The issue is not that the keeper can choose an unconfigured or foreign target. It is that [`_checkSourceConfig()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L378-L395) relates a source to the candidate only through `(Farm, targetPoolId)`:

```solidity
if (
    config.target.stakingContract != target.stakingContract
        || config.target.poolIndex != target.poolIndex
) {
    revert TargetMismatch();
}
if (config.targetPoolId != targetPoolId) {
    revert TargetPoolMismatch(config.targetPoolId, targetPoolId);
}
```

For V4-style connectors, [`_positionPoolId()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/strategies/MultiFarmStrategy.sol#L440-L459) obtains a non-zero PoolId directly from the position manager. This lets a source configuration distinguish two pools managed by the same contract.

For V3-style connectors, the helper calls `positionPoolKey()` with a zero factory. [`UniswapV3Connector.positionPoolKey()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/connectors/uniswap/UniswapV3Connector.sol#L127-L140) can derive the position's actual pool address only by querying that factory and always returns a zero `poolId`:

```solidity
return NftPoolKey({
    poolAddress: IUniswapV3Factory(poolFactory).getPool(
        token0, token1, fee_
    ),
    poolId: bytes32(0) // Uniswap V4 only
});
```

The zero-factory call reverts and `_positionPoolId()` deliberately catches it and returns `bytes32(0)`. As a result, the pool address recovered by the connector is discarded and all V3 positions are represented by a zero `targetPoolId`.

This collapses the complete source-target key for a bare V3 manager. The Uniswap V3 test fixture uses the same value `Farm{UniswapV3.NFT_MANAGER, 0}` for both the WBTC/WETH and USDC/WETH pools in [`test/strategies/UniswapV3.t.sol`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/test/strategies/UniswapV3.t.sol#L27-L38). Therefore, these two owner-written configurations are indistinguishable to `_checkSourceConfig()`:

```solidity
// A is a WBTC/WETH position; B is a USDC/WETH position.
config[A] = MultiCompoundConfig({
    enabled: true,
    target: Farm({ stakingContract: NFT_MANAGER, poolIndex: 0 }),
    targetPoolId: bytes32(0)
});
config[B] = MultiCompoundConfig({
    enabled: true,
    target: Farm({ stakingContract: NFT_MANAGER, poolIndex: 0 }),
    targetPoolId: bytes32(0)
});
```

With `A` supplied as a source and `B` as `depositPosition`, `B` passes its exact opt-in and zap binding, while `A` matches `B` on both values checked by `_checkSourceConfig()`. The keeper can supply the swaps needed to convert A's collected fees and rewards into B's pool assets, and the resulting liquidity is added to B.

The V4 regression suite establishes the opposite pool-level invariant. [`test_nft_compound_multiple_for_uniswapv4_reverts_when_source_pool_a_targets_pool_b()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/test/strategies/MultiCompoundV4Pools.t.sol#L298-L335) configures two positions under the same manager but in different pools and expects `TargetPoolMismatch`. The equivalent V3 call succeeds because both PoolIds have been normalized to zero.

Positions within the same correctly identified pool remain interchangeable if each carries a valid target opt-in. That is consistent with the current pool-level configuration model and is not the issue described here.

#### Impact

Low. The destination must be an individually owner-enabled NFT, and the operation moves only the selected sources' harvested fees and rewards rather than their principal. Nevertheless, the keeper can move those proceeds into a pool the source configuration cannot distinguish or exclude. This can change the assets and LP exposure in which the proceeds are reinvested — for example, converting fees intended for a WBTC/WETH target into USDC/WETH liquidity — despite the pool-level restriction enforced for equivalent V4 positions.

#### Recommendation

Carry a V3 pool identity in `MultiCompoundConfig` instead of treating zero as sufficient for every legacy NFT target. For example, add a separate target pool address:

```solidity
struct MultiCompoundConfig {
    bool enabled;
    Farm target;
    bytes32 targetPoolId; // V4-style targets
    address targetPool;   // V3-style targets; zero otherwise
}
```

For a V3 destination, `_checkDepositTarget()` should derive the candidate position's actual pool through its connector and require it to equal `config.targetPool`. `_checkSourceConfig()` should then compare each source's configured `targetPool` with the validated destination pool, just as it currently compares `targetPoolId` for V4. Alternatively, replace both fields with one domain-separated pool key that encodes either a V4 PoolId or a V3 pool address.

Do not require a destination `tokenId` unless source-to-specific-position routing is an intended product feature. Pool identity is sufficient to restore the invariant already enforced for V4 without introducing stale references when a target NFT is rebalanced and receives a new token ID.

#### Developer Response

Fixed in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#894, commits 7c517ee8 and f768c5cc.

- The destination declares its pool and every source must agree with it.
- A V3-style destination reports a zero `PoolId`, so leaving `targetPool` unset would make all sources compare equal. Such a config is now rejected at write time with `DestinationPoolNotDeclared`.
- The cleaner long-term fix is using the pool address as the farm identifier. That is a larger change and we have not made it.

`Automation` documents every `(strategy, selector)` registration as mapping an action to one permission bit, and the deployment tooling only produces such single-bit values. However, `setActionPermission()` accepts any non-zero `uint256`, including a composite mask. Authorization handles that composite as a set of required bits, while `GasAccount` treats it as one exact permission key. A malformed admin registration can therefore have different semantics across the two components.

#### Technical Details

[`actionPermission`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/Automation.sol#L156-L160) and [`setActionPermission()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/Automation.sol#L636-L651) both describe the registered value as a permission bit. The setter nevertheless stores any value without validating that it is zero or a power of two:

```solidity
function setActionPermission(
    address strategy,
    bytes4 selector,
    uint256 permission
) external onlyAdmin {
    actionPermission[strategy][selector] = permission;
    emit ActionPermissionSet(strategy, selector, permission);
}
```

Zero has an explicit meaning: [`_executeOne()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/Automation.sol#L739-L764) treats it as an unregistered action. Every other value is accepted. If an admin registers an action under a composite such as `MULTI_COMPOUND | FARM_HARVEST`, [`_requireAuthorized()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/Automation.sol#L876-L899) requires the owner and automator to hold both bits:

```solidity
if (actionConsent[sickle] & action != action) {
    revert ActionNotConsented(sickle, action);
}
// ...
if ((globalAutomatorPermissions[msg.sender] & action) != action) {
    revert NotApprovedAutomator();
}
```

The same composite is then forwarded unchanged to `GasAccount`. Unlike authorization, [`GasAccount._validateCadence()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/GasAccount.sol#L249-L273) and [`_recordCadence()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/GasAccount.sol#L275-L286) look up the whole value as one mapping key. Consequently, `cadenceRequired[MULTI_COMPOUND]` and `cadenceRequired[FARM_HARVEST]` may both be true while `cadenceRequired[MULTI_COMPOUND | FARM_HARVEST]` remains false. The composite action will not inherit either constituent bit's cadence.

The comment that permission masks are free-form in [`AutomationPermissions`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/contracts/libraries/AutomationPermissions.sol#L33-L36) applies to grant masks and permits future categories to use higher bits. It does not make composite action registrations part of the configured design: the same library says that each generic-dispatch pair is bound to a bit, and [`_automationActionSpec()`](https://github.com/vfat-io/sickle-contracts/blob/221a7d22fcc7a5fe2fb5ecc9e9ffe493b2353d7c/script/helpers/configuration.sol#L3185-L3275) resolves every supported action to exactly one named, one-hot permission.

The audited deployment plans therefore do not expose this state. It can only be introduced through a direct or future admin-controlled registration that violates the invariant already followed by the protocol tooling. The concern is configuration hardening: the contract accepts a representation that its documentation, tooling and exact-key cadence accounting do not consistently support. If an admin or future deployment script supplies a composite, cadence limits and monitoring configured for its constituent categories will not apply to that action.

#### Impact

Informational.

#### Recommendation

Enforce the documented representation at the boundary. Preserve zero as the unregister value and reject every non-zero value that is not a power of two:

```solidity
error InvalidActionPermission(uint256 permission);

function setActionPermission(
    address strategy,
    bytes4 selector,
    uint256 permission
) external onlyAdmin {
    if (permission != 0 && permission & (permission - 1) != 0) {
        revert InvalidActionPermission(permission);
    }
    actionPermission[strategy][selector] = permission;
    emit ActionPermissionSet(strategy, selector, permission);
}
```

#### Developer Response

Fixed in vfat-io/sickle-contracts#895, landed on `main` via #891, commit b21233d3.

- `setActionPermission()` rejects composite masks; the permission must be a single bit. Zero still means unregistered.
